  <main class="main">

    <!-- ── NOTE FROM LINDA ── -->
    <div class="card" id="note">
      <div class="card-banner banner-navy">
        <div class="banner-step" style="color:rgba(245,200,66,0.8);">Start Here</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">✍️</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title" style="color:var(--gold);">A Note From Linda</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">Why this resource exists</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number" style="color:rgba(245,200,66,0.08);">✦</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>This resource was built from 15+ years as a Dyslexia Specialist and Orton-Gillingham practitioner -- working with students that traditional classroom instruction did not reach. I am not a classroom teacher. I am the person educators call when their students are still struggling. That perspective is exactly why this playbook exists.</p>
        <p>I am also a certified AI consultant and implementation specialist. I build AI-powered learning tools and use AI every single day in my practice. And I am telling you -- from the inside -- that <strong>AI alone will never close the learning gap</strong>. Because the gap is never just about skills. Underneath every skill gap is a belief gap.</p>
        <div class="callout">This is not a research document. It is a practitioner's framework built from real-world work with struggling learners of all ages. Some tools here will fit your context immediately. Others may need adapting. Take what serves your students and leave the rest.</div>
        <p>I hold certifications across three pillars: dyslexia and reading intervention, master-level NLP executive coaching, and AI consulting and implementation. I was one of five people who architected North Carolina House Bill 149, signed into law July 20, 2017 -- the first dyslexia legislation in the state of North Carolina.</p>
        <p style="font-style:italic;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);">I don't sell AI hype. I build with it. -- Linda Colasanti</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART ONE ── -->
    <div class="card" id="part1">
      <div class="card-banner banner-red">
        <div class="banner-step">Part One</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">🔍</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">Understanding the Real Problem</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">AI did not create this crisis</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">01</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>Every higher education leader is watching the same scene play out. A student who seemed engaged in class submits work that is clearly not their own. A brilliant mind sits in the back of the room, hood up, phone out, completely disengaged.</p>
        <div class="callout">AI did not create the literacy gap. It revealed it -- and gave students a faster way to hide it.</div>
        <p>The student who uses ChatGPT to write their paper did not develop that habit in your classroom. They developed it somewhere around second or third grade, when reading got hard and nobody had the right answer. By the time they arrived at your institution they had spent a decade perfecting the art of looking like they don't care -- because not caring hurts less than trying and failing.</p>
        <p>That is not an engagement problem. That is a belief and identity wound that has been following that student for fifteen years. <strong>And it just landed in your classroom.</strong></p>
        <p>Underneath every skill gap is a belief gap. A student who believes they are not capable will not become capable -- no matter how sophisticated your intervention or how powerful your technology. Fixing the skill without addressing the belief is like patching a roof without fixing the foundation.</p>
        <p>Before you write a student off -- ask yourself: Have I considered that this student might be embarrassed? That reading aloud in class might be their worst nightmare? That their silence is protection, not defiance?</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART TWO ── -->
    <div class="card" id="part2">
      <div class="card-banner banner-blue">
        <div class="banner-step">Part Two</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">🔄</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">The Flip</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">Assign AI outside. Prove mastery inside.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">02</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>The most common institutional response to AI in the classroom is detection and punishment. This approach has one fundamental problem: it positions you in a permanent arms race with technology that will always be one step ahead.</p>
        <div class="callout">Stop fighting AI. Start using it strategically. Flip the script -- and take your classroom back.</div>

        <p><strong>Step 1: Assign AI as the learning tool outside class.</strong> Rather than banning AI from homework, assign it intentionally. Give students a specific AI-assisted task designed to help them learn the material -- not just produce an output.</p>
        <p>Examples that work: Have students use Claude or ChatGPT to generate a first draft of an argument, then ask them to identify three things the AI got wrong. Ask them to prompt AI to argue the opposite side of their thesis and then write a rebuttal. Have them use AI to summarize a reading -- then find the errors.</p>
        <p>That last one is particularly powerful. AI makes mistakes -- confidently. A student who actually read the material will catch them instantly. A student who relied entirely on AI will not. <strong>You have just created a natural integrity check without accusation.</strong></p>

        <p><strong>Step 2: Prove mastery inside the classroom.</strong> Whatever the AI-assisted assignment produces outside class, the student must be able to defend, discuss, and demonstrate inside class. In real time. In front of you. Where nothing can be faked.</p>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong>Why this works:</strong> Students who used AI to actually learn the material will perform well. Students who used AI to avoid learning will be exposed naturally -- without accusation, without AI detection software, without confrontation. Your domain expertise becomes irreplaceable again. No AI can replicate what you know about your field at the depth you know it.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART THREE ── -->
    <div class="card" id="part3">
      <div class="card-banner banner-green">
        <div class="banner-step">Part Three</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">🎯</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">The Three Questions</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">What reveals real mastery instantly</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">03</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>You do not need a lengthy oral exam. You need three well-placed questions that make it immediately clear whether a student understands the material -- or just knows how to prompt a language model. These work across every subject and every level.</p>
        <p style="font-style:italic;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);">Delivery note: Ask these as genuine curiosity, not interrogation. The student who is unprepared will feel it immediately. The student who is prepared will feel respected. Tone is everything.</p>

        <div class="question-list">
          <div class="question-item">
            <div class="question-num">1</div>
            <div>
              <div class="question-text">"Walk me through why you made the argument you did -- not what the argument is, but why that one."</div>
              <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);margin-top:0.5rem;font-weight:600;">AI can produce an argument. It cannot explain the reasoning process behind choosing that argument. Listen for: evidence of independent thought, awareness of alternatives rejected.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="question-item">
            <div class="question-num">2</div>
            <div>
              <div class="question-text">"AI makes mistakes about this topic. What did you notice that was wrong or incomplete in what it produced?"</div>
              <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);margin-top:0.5rem;font-weight:600;">This assumes AI use -- removing shame and accusation entirely. A student who read the source material will have no trouble. Listen for: specific, accurate identification of a genuine gap or error.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="question-item">
            <div class="question-num">3</div>
            <div>
              <div class="question-text">"If you had to apply this concept to [a specific real-world scenario from your field], what would that actually look like?"</div>
              <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.5);margin-top:0.5rem;font-weight:600;">Fill in the scenario from your own deep knowledge -- something a generalist AI handles superficially. This is where your expertise becomes the irreplaceable measuring stick. Listen for: specificity, nuance, field-awareness.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout">You do not need all three every time. One well-placed question mid-discussion can tell you what you need to know about an entire class's engagement level.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART FOUR ── -->
    <div class="card" id="part4">
      <div class="card-banner banner-violet">
        <div class="banner-step">Part Four</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">👁️</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">The Invisible Struggler</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">What disengagement actually looks like</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">04</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>Not every student who reaches for AI is lazy. Some are hiding something that has nothing to do with laziness -- and everything to do with shame. After 15+ years working with the students classrooms could not reach, I can tell you that the most common reason a capable person stops engaging is not lack of effort. It is the belief that effort will only confirm what they already suspect about themselves.</p>

        <p><strong>Signs to watch for:</strong></p>
        <div class="question-list">
          <div class="question-item"><div class="question-num" style="font-size:1rem;width:28px;height:28px;background:rgba(168,85,247,0.2);border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--violet);">•</div><div class="question-text">Avoids reading aloud or presenting from text</div></div>
          <div class="question-item"><div class="question-num" style="font-size:1rem;width:28px;height:28px;background:rgba(168,85,247,0.2);border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--violet);">•</div><div class="question-text">Verbal participation is strong but written work is significantly weaker</div></div>
          <div class="question-item"><div class="question-num" style="font-size:1rem;width:28px;height:28px;background:rgba(168,85,247,0.2);border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--violet);">•</div><div class="question-text">Sudden increase in AI-polished writing that does not match their verbal voice</div></div>
          <div class="question-item"><div class="question-num" style="font-size:1rem;width:28px;height:28px;background:rgba(168,85,247,0.2);border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;color:var(--violet);">•</div><div class="question-text">Engages well in conversation but struggles to translate ideas to written form</div></div>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top:1rem;"><strong>What you can do:</strong> Create a dignified private pathway. A quiet conversation. A discreet referral. A resource they can access on their own terms without anyone knowing they used it.</p>
        <div class="callout">Reading For All is a private, device-based structured literacy community where adult learners can access foundational reading instruction without embarrassment. No one needs to know. Everyone is on their device. If you have a student you suspect is struggling at a foundational level -- there is somewhere dignified to send them now. readingforall.com</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PART FIVE ── -->
    <div class="card" id="part5">
      <div class="card-banner banner-gold">
        <div class="banner-step">Part Five</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">⏱️</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">AI as Your Tool</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">Save 5 hours a week on grading and lesson prep</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">05</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>Most conversations about AI in higher education focus on what students are doing with it. Almost none focus on what professors could be doing with it. You have deep domain expertise that took decades to build. AI should be amplifying that expertise -- not threatening it.</p>
        <p>Here is where your time goes and how AI reclaims it:</p>
        <div class="note-box">
          <strong>Rubric generation:</strong> Describe your assignment and ask Claude to generate a detailed rubric. Review and adjust. What used to take an hour takes ten minutes.<br/><br/>
          <strong>Assignment variation:</strong> Use AI to generate multiple versions of the same assignment so identical AI outputs across students become immediately obvious.<br/><br/>
          <strong>Discussion questions:</strong> Give AI your week's reading list and ask it to generate questions at multiple cognitive levels -- recall, analysis, synthesis, application.<br/><br/>
          <strong>Feedback templates:</strong> Create a library of feedback templates for the most common issues in student writing. Build them once, customize with student-specific details.<br/><br/>
          <strong>Syllabus and policy language:</strong> Draft or update your AI use policy, syllabus language, and course overview in minutes.
        </div>
        <div class="callout">Important: AI is a starting point, not a final product. Your expertise, your voice, and your judgment are what make the output useful. Review everything AI produces before using it. Your students are relying on your deep knowledge -- not the model's.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PROMPT LIBRARY ── -->
    <div class="card" id="prompts">
      <div class="card-banner banner-teal">
        <div class="banner-step">Part Six</div>
        <div class="banner-icon">📋</div>
        <div class="banner-text">
          <div class="banner-title">The Prompt Library</div>
          <div class="banner-sub">Click any prompt to copy -- paste directly into Claude or ChatGPT</div>
        </div>
        <div class="banner-number">06</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <p>These prompts are designed for professors and higher ed leaders. The more specific you are about your subject, level, and objectives, the better the output. <strong>Claude</strong> (claude.ai) tends to be stronger on nuanced writing and structured frameworks. <strong>ChatGPT</strong> tends to be stronger on rapid brainstorming and variation generation.</p>

        <div class="section-label" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Assignment Design</div>
        <div class="section-sub">Restructure assignments so AI becomes a learning tool, not a shortcut</div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Flip-the-Script Assignment</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p1">I want to redesign this assignment so that students use AI as a learning tool outside class and must demonstrate mastery inside class. Here is my original assignment: [paste prompt]. Please suggest how to restructure it using this framework: (1) an AI-assisted task students complete before class that requires genuine engagement with the material, (2) three in-class questions I can use to verify real understanding, and (3) a brief reflection prompt that asks students to identify one thing AI got wrong or incomplete.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p1', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Integrity-Proof Assessment</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p2">I teach [subject] at the graduate level. I want to create an assessment that would be very difficult to complete using AI alone without genuine domain knowledge. The learning objectives are: [list]. Please suggest three assessment formats and provide one complete example prompt for the strongest option.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p2', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="section-label" style="margin-top:2rem;">Grading and Feedback</div>
        <div class="section-sub">Save hours -- let AI draft, you refine</div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Rubric Builder</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p3">I am assigning [type of assignment] in a [level] [subject] course. The key learning objectives are: [list]. Please build a detailed rubric with [number] categories. For each category include: the category name, what distinguished performance looks like at four levels (exemplary, proficient, developing, beginning), and the point value if the total is [X] points.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p3', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Oral Defense Question Generator</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p4">A student has submitted the following paper abstract: [paste abstract]. I want to ask them 5 questions in an oral defense that will reveal whether they genuinely understand their argument and sources. Please generate 5 questions that progress from recall to analysis to original synthesis. Include one question that asks them to identify a limitation or error in their own argument.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p4', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Constructive Feedback Templates</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p5">I frequently need to give feedback on the following issues in graduate student writing: [list 3 common issues, e.g. weak thesis statements, over-reliance on quotation, lack of original argument]. Please write three separate feedback paragraphs I can use as templates, each 3-4 sentences, that are constructive and specific without being discouraging.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p5', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="section-label" style="margin-top:2rem;">Classroom Culture and Student Support</div>
        <div class="section-sub">Create conditions where AI becomes a tool, not a shortcut</div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
          <div class="prompt-label">Syllabus AI Policy Language</div>
          <div class="prompt-text" id="p6">I need to update my syllabus to include a clear, fair, and specific policy on AI use. My course is [subject] at the [level] level. My position is that AI may be used for [specific purposes] but not for [specific purposes]. Please draft a 150-word policy statement that is clear, firm, and non-punitive in tone. Include one sentence about how I will verify understanding in class.</div>
          <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyPrompt('p6', this)">📋 Copy Prompt</button>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-card">
